WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
Okay, let's dive into this explainer with a hard truth.

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.000
9 out of 10 safety stock numbers I see, they're completely wrong.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.000
And honestly, it's always for the exact same reason.

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Most teams are literally only using half the formula.

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.000
They calculate their buffer based on demand variability alone.

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
You know, z times sigma, demand times the root of lead time.

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
But that assumes your lead time basically never changes.

8
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.000
And if you're on GCC import lanes,

9
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
lead time is the absolute least constant thing you have.

10
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.000
To get the full equation,

11
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.000
you've got to use Grizzly's combined model and include both terms.

12
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.000
You must add your average demand multiplied by your lead time variability.

13
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.000
If you drop that second half on a volatile China to Jebla Alley lane,

14
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000
you are going to under buffer every single time.

15
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
The intuition here is actually pretty simple.

16
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000
If your supplier is reliable, you protect against demand.

17
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
If your demand is steady, but the port is a total mess,

18
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.000
you protect against the lane.

19
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
In the real world, you almost always have to do both.

20
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
And this brings us to a massive lever that basically nobody pulls.

21
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.000
You will actually cut your safety stock way faster

22
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.000
by reducing lead time variance

23
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.000
rather than just shrinking your lead time average.

24
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Seriously, chase consistency before you chase speed.

25
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.000
So stop guessing and run the real math for yourself.

26
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.000
There is a free safety stock calculator out there

27
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
that includes both full demand and lead time variance.

28
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
And yeah, absolutely zero sign up required.

29
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
It was built by Vinayak Bhadani in Dubai.

30
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Go grab it right now and ask yourself,

31
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.000
is your incomplete half formula quietly setting you up

32
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.000
for your next massive stock out?
